#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157086 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b0ef91-c5ed-5e5d-a777-dea3ae515aed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Intersection of Sets Without a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "37d8d7b3-4252-5b87-899a-655137acdb4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7156" :question/problem "Given the sets $X = \\{ 2, 5, 7, 8 \\},$ $Y = \\{ 1, 3, 5 \\},$ and $Z = \\{ 2, 4, 6, 8 \\},$ find $X \\cap Y,$ $Y \\cap Z,$ and $Z \\cap X.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $X$ and $Y.$ Since $X$ and $Y$ have only the element $5$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cap Y & =\\{2,5,7,8\\}\\cap \\{1,3,5\\} \\\\  & =\\{5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Y \\cap Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Y$ and $Z.$ Since $Y$ and $Z$ have no elements in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y\\cap Z & =\\{1,3,5\\}\\cap \\{2,4,6,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Z \\cap X$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Z$ and $X.$ Since $Z$ and $X$ have only the elements $2$ and $8$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z\\cap X & =\\{2,4,6,8\\}\\cap \\{2,5,7,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157087 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6a1874-a11e-58d6-8509-4cef2203fc64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Intersections of Sets Given Verbally"}] :question/id #uuid "a30b2847-895f-5a76-9e1d-dad43f528482" :question/math-academy-id "e-7157" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0,$ respectively. What is $A \\cap B?$" :question/worked-solution "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{ 6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cap B$ is the *intersection* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $A$ and $B.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & =\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,…\\}\\cap \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{12,24,36,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157094 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "953e79cf-c216-5d4b-a056-455f76e7fa87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Intersection of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "b33222a9-91db-51fd-b275-a6b593e2f6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7169" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{ 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{ 1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $X \\cap Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ consists of all the elements that are in both $X$ and $Y.$ To represent $X \\cap Y,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $X$ and $Y.$\n\n![](images/37/37243a3d1954c0ae7939c09ad05b6aa12a8dff8a3ae8915bbe2079051466cbcb.png)\n\nThe elements $1,$ $2,$ and $8$ are in both $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the intersection of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\{ 1,2,8 \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "953e79cf-c216-5d4b-a056-455f76e7fa87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Intersection of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3b5469-f2f7-5ec9-af05-9eb31e486da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{8,10,11\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557889} {:answer/id #uuid "55ea5f89-0f8b-5169-964c-acb156f06a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{8,10,11,12,15\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558026} {:answer/id #uuid "ba19e6bd-df23-5ee3-8975-fdd2a650c6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{10,12,15\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558241} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4f6151-17b9-5373-865a-e5fbbd3f4396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{10\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558243} {:answer/id #uuid "cb78322b-d735-5106-a745-0c38e9937c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{8,11,12,15\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc4f6151-17b9-5373-865a-e5fbbd3f4396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{10\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558243} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d160f10-0546-5e11-ac66-cd29ba0cf2a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d9c31b4-a0e3-5839-94dc-af5a271c4a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102077" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e495695d9def7518780fb2b9e44f11e890da4c70c2dff7c6525936b2684219e1.png)\n\nGiven the sets $S=\\{8,10,11\\}$ and $T=\\{10,12,15\\},$ find $S\\cap T.$" :question/worked-solution "The intersection $S\\cap T$ consists of all the elements that are in both $S$ and $T.$ To represent $S\\cap T,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $S$ and $T.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f807aaf7a026cc52f53f4a5f50afab269da5f2dfdcb835f6166891ada864f72.png)\n\nOnly the element $10$ is in both $S$ and $T.$ Therefore, the intersection of $S$ and $T$ is\n\n$$\nS\\cap T=\\{10\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "953e79cf-c216-5d4b-a056-455f